this has the same problem actually. We call the handlers before nulling the connection, so that when someone calls in they get the state of the connection that they just overwrote with null.


in general, I don't totally get why this method flows the way it does. early returns, roundabout ownership, etc.. It feels like it was refactored three times - it might be an opportunity to scour it completely and start anew!

could you do this instead?

```c++
void Connection(C newConn)
{
    auto oldState = ConnectionState(); // rely on ControlCore's automatic null handling
    // revoke ALL old handlers immediately

    _connection = newConn;
    if (_connection) {
        // do all hookups now, add ALL new handlers immediately, set window etc
    }

    if (oldState != ConnectionState()) { // rely on the null handling again
        // send the notification
    }
}
```
